<title>PORTUGAL: Portugal plans to outlaw transport blockades.</title>
<headline>Portugal plans to outlaw transport blockades.</headline>
<dateline>LISBON 1997-06-19</dateline>
<text>
<p>Portugal's Socialist government on Thursday approved a plan making the blockading of public means of transport during protests punishable by jail.</p>
<p>The draft law would allow for prison terms of up to three years for anybody impeding transport by land, sea or air regardless of whether their action led to damage or injury.</p>
<p>The measure could be used in the case of lorry strikes such as that which crippled France late last year. It would also apply in such cases as the blockading of the port of Lisbon earlier this month by angry fishing boat owners.</p>
<p>The trawler owners were protesting at the government's decision to ban fishing in certain parts of the coast for 24 hours at weekends.</p>
<p>The bill still has to go before parliament where the Socialists are four seats short of an overall majority in the 230-seat legislature.</p>
